  "lattice": "<-(sqrt(2)-1)> + Lambda22",
  "proof": "The integral isometry g preserves the chosen time sheet and maps v0 to gv0. If a reflective mirror separated these points, its Vinberg height would be at most 56+40*sqrt(2) and it would pair positively with z=(1,d/3). The complete H<=150 frontier certificate excludes such a root. The complete norm alphabet also excludes mirrors with time coordinate zero, so v0 is regular. Therefore v0 and gv0 lie in the same reflection chamber C. Every integral isometry permutes the complete set of primitive reflective roots, so g normalizes the reflection group and maps chambers to chambers; hence gC=C. The arithmetic orthogonal group is discrete and g has infinite order. Since the plane trace of g is >2, g is a hyperbolic translation. Proper discontinuity gives a small ball U whose distinct g-powers are pairwise disjoint. As gC=C, choosing U inside C puts every equal-volume translate g^nU inside C. Thus C has infinite volume. Equivalently, the standard reflection-chamber action identifies the chamber stabilizer with the quotient of the arithmetic group by its normal reflection subgroup; the infinite stabilizer gives infinite index.",
